Q: Eclipse can&#39;t find XML related classes after switching build path to JDK 10 A: <p>The scan_deps script below worked for me two find the culprit classpath entries. I removed or excluded them one by one.</p>
<p>Example output:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/nSU8ms3P.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/nSU8ms3P.png" alt="scan_deps output example" /></a></p>
<pre><code>scan_deps               
➜ Building Maven classpath to scan for: org.w3c.dom.Document
➜ Scanning JARs...

⚠ FOUND in: /Users/wf/.m2/repository/jtidy/jtidy/4aug2000r7-dev/jtidy-4aug2000r7-dev.jar
➜   (Generating full dependency tree once for lookup...)
    Dependency Tree Context:
    +- com.bitplan.java:com.bitplan.testutil:jar:0.1.2:compile
    |  \- httpunit:httpunit:jar:1.7:compile
    |     +- javax.servlet:servlet-api:jar:2.3:compile
    |     \- jtidy:jtidy:jar:4aug2000r7-dev:compile

✗ Error: Scan Complete. Found 1 colliding artifacts.

⚠ Debug tip: Full dependency tree saved to /tmp/mvn_full_tree_cache.txt
</code></pre>
<p>until</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/JfG6adc2.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/JfG6adc2.png" alt="success image" /></a></p>
<pre><code>scan_deps
➜ Building Maven classpath to scan for: org.w3c.dom.Document
➜ Scanning JARs...

✓ Scan Complete. Class 'org.w3c.dom.Document' not found.
</code></pre>
<p><strong>scan_deps</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>#!/bin/bash
# WF 2026-01-21
# Scan Maven dependencies for duplicate classes and trace their POM origin.
#
# Usage:
#   ./scan_split_packages.sh [class_name]

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Messaging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
blue='\033[0;34m'
red='\033[0;31m'
green='\033[0;32m'
yellow='\033[1;33m'
magenta='\033[0;35m'
endColor='\033[0m'

color_msg() { echo -e &quot;${1}${2}${endColor}&quot; &gt;&amp;&quot;${3:-1}&quot;; }
error()     { color_msg &quot;$red&quot; &quot;✗ Error: $1&quot; 2; [ &quot;${2:-1}&quot; -ne 0 ] &amp;&amp; exit &quot;${2:-1}&quot;; }
success()   { color_msg &quot;$green&quot; &quot;✓ $1&quot;; }
action()    { color_msg &quot;$blue&quot; &quot;➜ $1&quot;; }
warn()      { color_msg &quot;$yellow&quot; &quot;⚠ $1&quot;; }
info()      { color_msg &quot;$magenta&quot; &quot;  $1&quot;; }

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Functions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

usage() {
  echo &quot;Usage: $0 [classname]&quot;
  exit 1
}

# Convert java class to file path
notate_class() {
  local l_input=&quot;$1&quot;
  local l_path=&quot;${l_input//.//}&quot;
  [[ &quot;$l_path&quot; != *&quot;.class&quot; ]] &amp;&amp; l_path=&quot;${l_path}.class&quot;
  echo &quot;$l_path&quot;
}

#
# Extract Artifact ID safely by directory walking
# The .m2 structure is ALWAYS: .../repository/[group]/[artifact]/[version]/[file.jar]
#
extract_artifact_id() {
  local l_jarpath=&quot;$1&quot;

  # 1. Get the directory containing the jar (this is the VERSION folder)
  local l_version_dir
  l_version_dir=$(dirname &quot;$l_jarpath&quot;)

  # 2. Get the parent of that (this is the ARTIFACT folder)
  local l_artifact_dir
  l_artifact_dir=$(dirname &quot;$l_version_dir&quot;)

  # 3. The name of that folder is the artifactId
  basename &quot;$l_artifact_dir&quot;
}

#
# Generate the Validation Tree (Single Pass Cache)
#
CACHE_FILE=&quot;/tmp/mvn_full_tree_cache.txt&quot;
ensure_tree_cache() {
  if [ ! -f &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot; ]; then
    action &quot;  (Generating full dependency tree once for lookup...)&quot;
    # We dump the whole tree to a file. It is safer to Grep locally than ask Maven to filter.
    mvn dependency:tree -DoutputFile=&quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot; -q
  fi
}

#
# Find provenance using the cached tree
#
find_dependency_provenance() {
  local l_artifact_id=&quot;$1&quot;

  # 1. Make sure we have the tree
  ensure_tree_cache

  # 2. Search for the artifact in the text file
  # We search for &quot;:artifactId:&quot; style patterns to avoid partial matches
  info &quot;  Dependency Tree Context:&quot;

  # We grep for the artifact, attempting to match typical Maven tree output formatting
  # e.g., &quot;+- group:artifact:jar:ver&quot;
  if grep -q &quot;:${l_artifact_id}:&quot; &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot;; then
    # Print the matching line and the 3 lines preceding it to show the parent
    grep -B 3 &quot;:${l_artifact_id}:&quot; &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot; | grep -v &quot;\-\-&quot; | while read -r line; do
       info &quot;  $line&quot;
    done
  else
    # Fallback to loose search if strict ID match fails
    if grep -q &quot;${l_artifact_id}&quot; &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot;; then
       grep -B 2 &quot;${l_artifact_id}&quot; &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot; | grep -v &quot;\-\-&quot; | while read -r line; do
         info &quot;  $line&quot;
       done
    else
       warn &quot;  (Artifact found on disk but not in 'mvn dependency:tree'. It might be a transitive test scoped dep?)&quot;
    fi
  fi
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main Logic
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

target_class=&quot;org.w3c.dom.Document&quot;
[ -n &quot;$1&quot; ] &amp;&amp; target_class=&quot;$1&quot;
search_path=$(notate_class &quot;$target_class&quot;)

# Cleanup previous cache
rm -f &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot;

if [ ! -f &quot;pom.xml&quot; ]; then
  error &quot;No pom.xml found in current directory.&quot;
fi

action &quot;Building Maven classpath to scan for: $target_class&quot;
l_classpath=$(mvn dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=/dev/stdout -q 2&gt;/dev/null)

if [ -z &quot;$l_classpath&quot; ]; then
  error &quot;Maven compilation failed or returned empty classpath.&quot;
fi

action &quot;Scanning JARs...&quot;
IFS=':' read -ra l_jars &lt;&lt;&lt; &quot;$l_classpath&quot;

l_count=0
l_found=0

for l_jar in &quot;${l_jars[@]}&quot;; do
  if [ -f &quot;$l_jar&quot; ]; then
    # Check inside the zip
    if unzip -l &quot;$l_jar&quot; 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -q &quot;$search_path&quot;; then
       echo &quot;&quot;
       warn &quot;FOUND in: $l_jar&quot;

       # 1. Get ID from directory structure (Rock solid for .m2)
       l_artifact_id=$(extract_artifact_id &quot;$l_jar&quot;)

       # 2. Look up in the tree
       find_dependency_provenance &quot;$l_artifact_id&quot;

       ((l_found++))
    fi
  fi
done

echo &quot;&quot;
if [ &quot;$l_found&quot; -gt 0 ]; then
  # Don't delete the cache so user can inspect manualy if needed
  # rm -f &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot;
  error &quot;Scan Complete. Found $l_found colliding artifacts.&quot; 0
  echo &quot;&quot;
  warn &quot;Debug tip: Full dependency tree saved to $CACHE_FILE&quot;
else
  success &quot;Scan Complete. Class '$target_class' not found.&quot;
  rm -f &quot;$CACHE_FILE&quot;
fi

</code></pre>

Q: Correct syntax for function template with an even number of `int` arguments? A: <p>Following @Jarod42's answer, it is possible to avoid one level of <code>{}</code> at call site, i.e. one could have:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int main ()
{
    // Expecting 0.0234
    printf (&quot;x = %f\n&quot;, xfun (0.1, {2,2}, {3,3}, {4,4}));

    // Expecting 0.56
    printf (&quot;x = %f\n&quot;, xfun (0.1, {5,1}, {6,2}));
}
</code></pre>
<p>For doing this, we have to provide to <code>xfun</code> variadic arguments that are <code>std::initializer_list</code> objects:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...T&gt;
double xfun (double p, std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;...l);
</code></pre>
<p>Inside <code>xfun</code> implementation, we can convert this list as an <code>std::array</code>:</p>
<pre><code>std::array&lt;Coeff,sizeof...(l)&gt; arr = {Coeff(l)...};
</code></pre>
<p>where we have added a constructor for <code>Coeff</code> that takes as input a <code>std::initializer_list</code>:</p>
<pre><code>struct Coeff  {
   Coeff (std::initializer_list&lt;int&gt; l) {
       // no constexpr 'size' for std::initializer_list in c++11 :(
       if (l.size()!=2)  { throw; }
       t  = *(l.begin()+0);
       ex = *(l.begin()+1);
   }
   int t;
   int ex;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then the full code of <code>xfun</code> is as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...T&gt;
double xfun (double p, std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;...l)  {
    // We build an array of Coeff object from the initializer lists.
    std::array&lt;Coeff,sizeof...(l)&gt; arr = {Coeff(l)...};
    double result=0.0;
    for (auto const&amp; c : arr) { result += c.t*std::pow(p,c.ex); }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/11bsEfhn8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>Still, having the coefficients in a vector as @Jarod42 proposed seems a better idead for many reasons (centralization, reuse,...)</p>

Q: How to add links in Excel A: <p>It seems the real question is how to create Excel files from data, with a hyperlink column.</p>
<h1>Exporting Excel files (XLSX)</h1>
<p>XLSX is the standard format since 2007. It's a ZIP package containing XML files with a well-documented format. Everyone can create them but it's a lot easier to use libraries commercial or open-source libraries like EPPlus, ClosedXML, Sylvan.Data.Excel, MiniExcel, NPOI etc. There's a tradeoff between features and speed, but all can easily create a workbook with data.</p>
<h2>EPPlus</h2>
<p>EPPlus is one of the most advanced  non-commercial libraries. It can <a href="https://github.com/EPPlusSoftware/EPPlus/wiki/Reading-and-Writing-Data#load-data-into-a-spreadsheet" rel="nofollow noreferrer">load data from multiple sources</a> including collections, DataReader, DataTable, etc, control formatting and formulas, create pivot tables etc.</p>
<p>Let's say you have a list (or any IEnumerable&lt;&gt;) of this class :</p>
<pre><code>public class MyClass
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Number { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can create a real Excel file with the data with just :</p>
<pre><code>using (var pck = new ExcelPackage())
{
    var sheet = pck.Workbook.Worksheets.Add(&quot;sheet&quot;);
    var range = sheet.Cells[&quot;C1&quot;].LoadFromCollection(items);

    pck.SaveAs(@&quot;c:\workbooks\myworkbook.xlsx&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or, you can include headers, specify the table style, and save to the stream created by SaveFileDialog :</p>
<pre><code>using (var stream =saveDialog.OpenFile())
{
    using (var pck = new ExcelPackage(stream))
    {
        var sheet = pck.Workbook.Worksheets.Add(&quot;sheet&quot;);
        var range = sheet.Cells[&quot;C1&quot;].LoadFromCollection(items,true, TableStyles.Dark1);

        pck.Save();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can export query results without first loading them into memory as objects with <code>LoadFromDataReader</code>. This can have a huge impact when exporting a lot of rows.</p>
<p>Hyperlink properties are <a href="https://github.com/EPPlusSoftware/EPPlus/wiki/LoadFromCollection#hyperlinks" rel="nofollow noreferrer">explicitly supported</a> , so this class will get loaded with an Edit column, without doing changing anything else:</p>
<pre><code>public class MyClass
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Number { get; set; }

    public ExcelHyperLink Edit =&gt; new ExcelHyperLink(
                $&quot;https://dev.azure.com/{Name}/_workitems/edit/{Id}&quot;, 
                Name
            );
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can control not just display but also totals and formulas <a href="https://github.com/EPPlusSoftware/EPPlus/wiki/LoadFromCollection-using-Attributes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">with attributes</a>. From the doc example, this class specifies two calculated columns, along with a lot of formatting :</p>
<pre><code>[EpplusTable(TableStyle = TableStyles.Dark1, PrintHeaders = true, AutofitColumns = true, AutoCalculate = true, ShowTotal = true, ShowFirstColumn = true)]
[
    EpplusFormulaTableColumn(Order = 6, NumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;, 
        Header = &quot;Tax amount&quot;, 
        FormulaR1C1 = &quot;RC[-2] * RC[-1]&quot;, 
        TotalsRowFunction = RowFunctions.Sum, TotalsRowNumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;),
    EpplusFormulaTableColumn(Order = 7, NumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;, 
        Header = &quot;Net salary&quot;, 
        Formula = &quot;E2-G2&quot;, 
        TotalsRowFunction = RowFunctions.Sum, TotalsRowNumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;)
]
public class Actor
{
    [EpplusIgnore]
    public int Id { get; set; }

    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 3)]
    public string LastName { get; set; }
    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 1, Header = &quot;First name&quot;)]
    public string FirstName { get; set; }
    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 2)]
    public string MiddleName { get; set; }

    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 0, NumberFormat = &quot;yyyy-MM-dd&quot;, TotalsRowLabel = &quot;Total&quot;)]
    public DateTime Birthdate { get; set; }

    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 4, NumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;, TotalsRowFunction = RowFunctions.Sum, TotalsRowNumberFormat = &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;)]
    public double Salary { get; set; }

    [EpplusTableColumn(Order = 5, NumberFormat = &quot;0%&quot;, TotalsRowFormula = &quot;Table1[[#Totals],[Tax amount]]/Table1[[#Totals],[Salary]]&quot;, TotalsRowNumberFormat =&quot;0 %&quot;)]
    public double Tax { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can probably create the hyperlink column as a formula too (I haven't tested it), with this formula</p>
<pre><code>HYPERLINK(&quot;https://dev.azure.com/&quot; &amp; B2 &amp; &quot;/_workitems/edit/&quot; &amp; A2, B2)
</code></pre>
<p>Eg :</p>
<pre><code>[EpplusFormulaTableColumn(
    Header = &quot;Edit&quot;, 
    Formula = &quot;HYPERLINK(\&quot;https://dev.azure.com/\&quot; &amp; B2 &amp; \&quot;/_workitems/edit/\&quot; &amp; A2, B2)&quot;)
]
public class MyClass
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Number { get; set; }

}
</code></pre>
<h2>ClosedXML</h2>
<p>ClosedXML is another library that can handle formulas and hyperlinks. It's a bit quirkier than EPPlus though. Data can be  <a href="https://github.com/ClosedXML/ClosedXML/wiki/Inserting-Data" rel="nofollow noreferrer">loaded from lists, tables etc</a> but formatting is trickier. <a href="https://github.com/ClosedXML/ClosedXML/wiki/Using-Hyperlinks" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hyperlinks can be added to individual cells</a> but that doesn't help in this question. <a href="https://github.com/ClosedXML/ClosedXML/wiki/Using-Formulas" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Formulas can be applied to ranges</a> though.</p>
<pre><code>var wb = new XLWorkbook();
var ws = wb.Worksheets.Add(&quot;Sheet1&quot;);

var rangeWithItems = ws.Cell(1, 1).InsertData(items.AsEnumerable());

rangeWithItems .LastColumn().FormulaR1C1 = &quot;HYPERLINK(\&quot;https://dev.azure.com/\&quot; &amp; RC[-3] &amp; \&quot;/_workitems/edit/\&quot; &amp; RC[-3], RC[-2])&quot;;

wb.SaveAs(&quot;Formulas.xlsx&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>R1C1 is a formula syntax with relative addressing that's also available in EPPlus.</p>
<h1>Exporting Text (CSV) files</h1>
<p>CSV isn't an Excel format. It's just a text file with Values Separated by Commas. It's a weak and ill-defined format. The name itself first appeared in 1983 and no real standard existed until 2005 with <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4180" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RFC 4180</a>. Even that doesn't specify any numeric or date format. Using <code>.</code> as the decimal separator and the ISO8601 format for dates (YYYY-MM-DD....) are common conventions.</p>
<h2>EPPlus</h2>
<p>In EPPlus you can use <a href="https://github.com/EPPlusSoftware/EPPlus/wiki/SaveToText" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SaveToText</a> to save to a text file (eg CSV, tab-delimited, fixed-width).</p>
<pre><code>using (var stream =saveDialog.OpenFile())
{
    using (var pck = new ExcelPackage())
    {
        var sheet = pck.Workbook.Worksheets.Add(&quot;sheet&quot;);
        var range = sheet.Cells[&quot;C1&quot;].LoadFromCollection(items);

        //Saving the range, not the sheet
        var format = new ExcelOutputTextFormat();
        range.SaveToText(stream, format);

    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Keep in mind that EPPlus isn't the fastest or most efficient CSV exporter. You're using a lot of resources to generate structures that can never be stored to text. The defaults can be a problem too.</p>
<h2>CsvHelper</h2>
<p><a href="https://joshclose.github.io/CsvHelper/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CsvHelper</a> is one of the most popular CSV libraries, offering many customization options. Writing records is as easy as calling <code>csv.WriteRecords</code></p>
<pre><code>using (var writer = new StreamWriter(&quot;path\\to\\file.csv&quot;))
using (var csv = new CsvWriter(writer, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))
{
    csv.WriteRecords(items);
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Sylvan.Data.Csv</h2>
<p>This library is one of the fastest and most efficient. This becomes very important when reading or writing lots of data or when you want to export query results directly to CSV without first</p>
<p>Again, writing is easy. The only difference is that <code>IEnumerable&lt;&gt;</code>s have to be wrapped in a DataReader wrapper.</p>
<pre><code>var itemsReader = items.AsDataReader();

using var csvWriter = CsvDataWriter.Create(&quot;demo.csv&quot;);
await csvWriter.WriteAsync(itemsReader);
</code></pre>
